@page "/download"
@inject WikiService Data

<PageTitle>Download · Hollowdeep Wiki</PageTitle>
<nav class="crumbs"><a href="">Home</a><span>›</span><span class="here">Download</span></nav>
<h1>Download</h1>
<p class="lead">This page always points at the newest release of Hollowdeep.</p>

<DownloadPanel />

<h2>Works on</h2>
<table class="info-table">
    <tr><th>Minecraft</th><td>Bedrock Edition <b>@Data.Release.Range</b></td></tr>
    <tr><th>Add-on version</th><td>@Data.Release.Version</td></tr>
    <tr><th>File</th><td>@Data.Release.File (@Data.Release.Size)</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Older versions</th><td><a href="@Data.Release.AllReleases">All releases on GitHub</a></td></tr>
</table>
<p class="muted small">
    The lowest version is set by the scripting API the add-on uses, the highest is the newest Minecraft it was tested on.
    Newer Minecraft versions usually work too, but haven't been checked yet.
</p>

<h2>Install</h2>
<ol class="plain-steps">
    <li>Download the <b>.mcaddon</b> file above and open it. Minecraft imports both the behavior pack and the resource pack.</li>
    <li>Edit your world, open <b>Behavior Packs</b>, and activate <b>Hollowdeep BP</b>. The resource pack is added with it.</li>
    <li>No experiments or Beta APIs are needed: Hollowdeep only uses stable scripting APIs.</li>
    <li>On a server, every player and the server need the same version.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Updating a world</h2>
<p>Updates keep the same pack IDs and item IDs, so you can drop the new version onto an existing world or server. Each release has a new version number, so Minecraft always imports it over the old one.</p>
<p>Rifts open when you update are cleaned up when the world loads, and anyone inside is sent home.</p>
